  "notes": "The research states this one as a rule rather than a description — \"a country-timbre-on-snap-grid rule\" — and that is exactly how it is authored: the kit is untouched ColliPark snap and the *lead* is the departure. `timbreHint` is `banjo_and_tele_riff`, `session.scales` leads with `minor_pentatonic` for the banjo/guitar-flavoured figure, and `melody.register` drops to [66, 88] because a plucked string does not live where a snap bell lives — the synth bell the research puts *over* it is the `countermelody`, which is why that block runs +12 and carries `octave_echo` first. The hand-clap and snap layers \"doubled in the chorus\" are `percs.densityPerBar` reaching 4 with the snap and tambourine lanes; the occasional octave figure under the hook is `bass808.slideProb` 0.14 on a single `P8` at `phrase_end`. ⚠ **Confidence is medium and the research says which half is which**: the chart placement and the ColliPark relationship are documented; the tempi are [LOW-CONF], so `session.bpm` 144–164 is the inference, as are the drum probabilities. ⛔ **This model covers the 2005–07 snap lane only.** The research is explicit that his 2001–03 records were made by a producer assigned to another genre, so nothing of that era is in here — `era` says 2005–2007 and means it. ⚠ The harmony is a static `i`; `i–VII` and `i–VI` are **INFERRED** at weights 3 and 2, and they are the first things to delete if this ever moves more than the records do. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
